So far, you've been using the PowerShell Console to call one command at a time. (Page 37)

For bigger projects, having to call each command individually is too time-consuming. 
Luckily, you can combine commands so you can call them as a single unit. You can use the PowerShell pipeline or saving your code in external scripts. 

Starting a Windows Service. 

  To illustrate why you'd want to combine commands, you'll start by doing a simple example the old way. You'll use two commands: Get-Service, which queries Windows 
  services and returns information about them; and Start-Service, which starts Windows services. 

  $serviceName = "wuauserv'
  Get-Service -Name $serviceName
  Start-Service -Name $serviceName

  You can run Get-Service just to make sure PowerShell doesn't throw any errors. Chances are the service is already running. 
  If it is, Start-service will simply return control to the console. 

  You can imagine how monotonous it might get if you were working with hundreds od services. 

Using the Pipeline

  The first way to simplify your code is by chaining together commands by using the PowerShell pipeline, a tool that allows you to send the output of one command
  directly to antoher command as input. To use the pipeline, use the pipe operator (|) between two commands like so:

  command1 | command2

  Here is the output of command1 is piped into command2, becoming command2's input. The final command in the pipeline will output to the console. (Page 38)
  Many shell scripting languages including cmd.exe and bash, use a pipeline. But what makes the pipeline in Powershell unique is that it passes objects and not simple    strings. 

Piping Objects between Commands

  To send the output of Get-Service into Start-Service: 

  Get-Service -Name 'wuauserv' | Start-Service

  In this example, you don't have to specify any parameters because PowerShell takes care of that for you. It looks at the output of Get-Service, decides what values   it should pass to Start-Service, and matches the value to the parameters that Start-Service takes. 

  You can could also run it without any parameters. 

  'wuauserv' | Get-Service | Start-Service

  Powershell sends the string wuauserv into Get-Service and the output of Get-Service into Start-Service - all without you having to specify a thing!
  You've combined three separate commands into a single line, but you'll still have to reenter that line for every service you want to start. 

Piping Arrays Between Commands

  If you create a text file in notepad called services.txt that contain the strings Wuauserve and W32Time on separates lines you have created a file with services you
  can run. 
  A file like this can contain as many services as you like. To disaply the file to your powershell window, use the Get-Content cmdlet's Path Parameter. (Page 39).

  Get-Content -Path C:\Services.txt

  The Get-Content command reads in a file line by line, adding each line to an array and then returning that array. 
  You can also use the pipeline to pass the array that Get-Contents returns into the Get-Service command. 

  Get-Content -Path C:\Services.txt | Get-Service

  The Get-Content command is reading in the text file and spitting out an array. 
  But instead of sending the array itself througgh the pipeline, PowerShell unwraps it, sending each item in the array individually through the pipeline. 

  This allows you to execute the same commands for every item in the array. By putting every service you want to start in your text file, and tacking on an extra
  | Start-Service you have a insgle comand that can start as many services as you need. 

  Get-Content -Path C:\Services.txt | Get-Service | Start-Service

  There's no limit to how many commands you can stitch together using the pipeline. But if you find yourself going over five, you may need to rethink your approach. 
  Note that although the pipeline is powefule, it won't work everywhere: most PowerShell commands accept only certain types of pipeline input, and some don't 
  accept any at all.

Looking at Parameter Binding 

  When you pass parameters to a command, PowerShell initiates a process known as parameter binding, in which it matches each object you pass into the command to the 
  various parameters specified by the command's creator. 

  For a Powershell command to accept pipeline input the person who writes the command -whether that's Microsoft or you has to explicitly build in pipeline support
  for one or more parameters. 
  You'll notice an error if you attempt to pipe information into a command that doesn't have pipeline support on any parameter, or if Powershell cannot find a 
  suitable binding. 

  'string' | Get-Process

  You should see that the command won't accept pipeline input. 
  To see if using the pipeline is even possible, you can look at a command's full help content by using the Full paramter on the Get-Help command. 

  Get-Help -Name Get-Service -Full

  You should get quite a bit of output. Scroll down to the PARAMETERS section. This section lists information about each parameter and gives you more information
  than you'd get by not using the Detailed or Full parameter at all. 

  There's a lot of information here, but the focus is on the Accept pipeline input. 
  This field tells you whether a parameter accepts a pipeline input or not; if the parameter didn't accept pipeline input, you'd see false next to this field.
  There can be even more information such as if the parameter accepts pipeline input both via ByValue and ByPropertyName. 

  -Name <string[]>

  -ComputerName <string[]>

  ComputerName only accpets ByPropertyName. (Page 41).

  
  
